    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with **Pete Crow-Armstrong** standing as our clear focal point. With an elite efficiency score of 80.21, he is the primary engine for our 5x5 objectives; his matchup against Pallante offers a significant platoon advantage, and critically, the SP\u2019s slow delivery to home plate maximizes **Pete Crow-Armstrong**\u2019s stolen base upside\u2014our most precious resource. **Caleb Durbin** also projects as a high-floor anchor at 67.73. Assuming he maintains his projected #5 spot against Mason Barnett, his efficiency is bolstered by a favorable park environment and a targetable SP, making him a cornerstone for counting stats. **Lars Nootbaar** similarly profiles well (62.26) against Assad, benefiting from both the handedness matchup and a strong spot in the heart of the order.\n\nNavigating the remainder of the lineup required tough decisions, particularly concerning **Carson Benge**. Despite a 48.48 score, he barely edged out our deeper reserves. The decision to start **Carson Benge** over the likes of **Josh Smith** or **Casey Schmitt** was driven by his clean-up position, though his outlook is tempered by a 35% rain risk that necessitates close monitoring as first pitch approaches. Meanwhile, **Junior Caminero** (57.51) and **Chase Meidroth** (47.84) are clear starts, though they face stiffer tests against Winn and Weathers, respectively. We continue to hold high-impact veterans like **Yordan Alvarez**, **Trea Turner**, **Kyle Schwarber**, and **Christian Yelich** on the bench due to scheduled off-days, forcing us to lean heavily on the speed and versatility of **Jacob Gonzalez**, who remains a marginal play at 51.30 while we wait for official confirmation of his position in the order. We are keeping **Hyeseong Kim**, **Kazuma Okamoto**, **Colt Keith**, **Nelson Velazquez**, **Gary Sanchez**, **Dillon Dingler**, **Pedro Ramirez**, and **Luis Pena** ready in the wings should weather or lineup shifts necessitate a late-cycle pivot.",
